<section class="blk">
  <h2>Archives identified and not yet worked</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><b>NAAIRS</b> — the National Automated Archival Information Retrieval System, South Africa. The
      single most important unopened door: it indexes <b>Cape (KAB) and Transvaal (TAB) estate files and
      death notices</b>, which is where a death notice for Willem Hermanus Booyzen would be found.</p>
    <p><b>Cape Archives (KAB), Master's Office (MOOC)</b> — one reference is already in hand:
      <span class="m">MOOC 6/9/35: 7698 (1845)</span>, the estate of George Augustus Kolbe. It has not been
      ordered or read.</p>
    <p><b>The Dutch Reformed Church registers</b> for Graaff-Reinet, Cradock, Colesberg, Burgersdorp,
      Dordrecht and Barkly East — already the backbone of the tree's citations, but read only where
      FamilySearch has indexed them.</p>
    <p><b>Anglo-Boer War records</b> — camp registers, Cape Rebel registers, Special Tribunal files, the
      list of the 44 executed for high treason, and the National Scouts rolls that would test the "joiners"
      half of the story.</p>
    <p><b>FindMyPast and FamilySearch</b> — both hold transcripts already cited in the tree (one FindMyPast
      transcript reference, <span class="m">R_177176049779</span>, is recorded against the 1812 birth) and
      neither has been systematically searched for this surname.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="lab"><span class="m">§ 12</span> · High priority</div>
    <h3 style="margin:8px 0 10px">Who is in the "Kolbe–Downing" photograph?</h3>
    <p style="font-size:16px">A carte-de-visite captioned as George Augustus Kolbe and Margaret Downing
      cannot be them — the costume is c. 1875–1885, and they died in 1844 and 1867. The likeliest sitter
      is their son <b>George Augustus Kolbe (b. 1836)</b>.</p>
    <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px"><strong>Where to look:</strong> the pencilled inscription on the
      card mount, at higher resolution than the tree holds; the photographer's imprint if the reverse
      survives; and the descendants who uploaded it. Also: who took it, and where.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="lab"><span class="m">§ 16</span> · High priority</div>
    <h3 style="margin:8px 0 10px">Who are the Mountjoys of Barkly Oost? — <em>ANSWERED</em></h3>
    <p style="font-size:16px">Found by accident, on the page opposite a Booyzen baptism: the
      <b>Doop Register, Barkly Oost, 6 October 1889</b> records <b>Hercules Johannes Mountjoy</b> and
      Maria Sophia van Niekerk baptising a son, with <b>Jakobus Jonathan Mountjoy</b> and
      <b>Maria Sophia Mountjoy</b> among the witnesses.</p>
    <p style="font-size:16px;margin-top:10px">This line descends from <b>Johanna Catharina Mountjoy</b>
      (1819–1863), of whom the archive has little more than a surname and a father who died in 1827.
      Here are three named Mountjoys in the right district, twenty-six years after her death.</p>
    <p style="font-size:16px;margin-top:10px"><span class="chip doc">Answered</span> <b>They are the
      descendants of Johanna Catharina Mountjoy's brother.</b> <b>George Mountjoy (b. 1823, Cradock),
      saddle maker</b>, married Maria Sophia Botha at Cradock in 1848 and had thirteen children.
      <b>Hercules Johannes</b> (the father at the 1889 font) and <b>James/Jakobus Jonathan</b> (the
      witness, who died at Barkly East in 1937) are two of his sons. The child baptised that day,
      <b>George Mountjoy (1889–1963)</b>, was a <b>second cousin</b> of the Booyzen daughter baptised in
      the same register three weeks earlier. <a href={u("/women/")}>The branch &rarr;</a></p>
    <p style="font-size:16px;margin-top:12px"><span class="chip doc">Now confirmed from originals</span>
      The parentage no longer rests on a user-edited tree. The <b>Dutch Reformed baptism register</b>
      records <b>Herculas Johannes Mountjoy, born 16 July 1866, baptised 29 July 1866, father George
      Mountjoy, mother Maria Sophia Botha</b>. His 1941 death registration gives his age as <b>74</b> —
      a man born in July 1866 is 74 in May 1941. His <b>marriage at Dordrecht on 8 October 1888, aged
      22</b>, is a year before the 1889 font. The chain closes.</p>
    <p style="font-size:16px;margin-top:10px"><b>James Jonathan is "James John."</b> His estate is
      <span class="m">KAB MOOC 6/9/5047, ref 58379 (1938)</span>, and his wife's is the very next file,
      <span class="m">58380</span> — Maria Louisa Bertha née Kumm. Their children's baptisms enter the
      father under both name forms and spell the mother's surname five ways (Kumm, Kuen, Kunn, Kum, Keem).</p>
    <p style="font-size:16px;margin-top:10px"><b>Hercules Johannes has no estate file at all.</b> The Cape
      Town Archives were searched under MOUNTJOY (42 documents; all 22 dated 1930 or later read in full),
      MONTJOY (4), and MOUNTJOY combined with HERKLAAS, HERCULES, WODEHOUSE and BARKLY (0 each). The
      estates around him run 1938, 1939, 1943, 1946, 1948 — an unbroken sequence, so this is an absence
      rather than a gap in the index. The likeliest reading is an estate too small to report.</p>
    <p style="font-size:16px;margin-top:10px"><span class="chip doc">Tested again, and it holds</span>
      The Cape probate collection has now been read for <b>every indexed MOUNTJOY at once</b> — 61
      personas, the whole surname in the whole collection. <b>Hercules Johannes returns exactly one
      hit, and it is his mother-in-law's.</b> His name is written on her 1902 death notice, in the list
      of her children, and the index keys every name on a page. The absence is real, and this is what
      it looks like from the inside. <a href={u("/documents/#van-niekerk-1902")}>Her notice &rarr;</a></p>
    <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px"><strong>What is left:</strong> the four small corrections for
      our own tree — <b>Sleer/Slier</b> vs Schleher (the 1863 death notice now settles this in favour of
      an <em>l</em>), the 1817 Graaff-Reinet marriage, Thomas Jacobus living to 1827, and the missing
      sibling <b>Jacoba Catharina (1820–1842)</b>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="lab"><span class="m">§ 15</span> · High priority</div>
    <h3 style="margin:8px 0 10px">Why was Kathleen Barry a "Military Pensioner"?</h3>
    <p style="font-size:16px">Her 1940 death certificate records her, at eighty-two, as a
      <b>Military Pensioner</b>. Her husband had been dead thirty-five years and died of a stomach ulcer.
      <b>13 September 2026 — one candidate found and rejected the same day.</b> A ninth son turned up in
      the Brixton burial register, RICHARD PETER BOOYZEN, dead at twenty-four on 25 October 1918, in the
      middle of an epidemic that killed thousands of South African servicemen and pensioned their
      dependants. His death registration says he was a MARRIED SAWYER of 18 Anderson Street, Turffontein,
      dead of influenza and pneumonia after eleven days. NOT A SOLDIER. The question stands, and is now
      sharper for it: the pension belongs to a son who SERVED AND SURVIVED.
      This archive guessed a <b>son killed in the First World War</b>, with a dependant's pension.</p>
    <p style="font-size:16px;margin-top:10px"><b>SEARCHED — and the guess is wrong.</b> The South Africa
      War Graves Project holds <b>no Booyzen</b> among the First World War dead (its two Booyzens died in
      1944 and 1993), and of <b>thirteen</b> Booysen deaths in 1914–18, <b>not one matches a son by
      forename</b> — and most are in the <b>Native Labour Corps</b>, <b>Cape Coloured Labour Regiment</b>
      and <b>Military Labour Bureau</b>, units not open to a family recorded as "European" on every
      certificate this archive has read. Decisively: <b>all her identifiable sons survived</b> — dying in
      1932, 1952, 1956, 1966 and 1980.</p>
    <p style="font-size:16px;margin-top:10px">So the pension belongs to a man who <b>served and came
      home</b> — a disabled son with his mother as dependant, or another entitlement altogether.</p>
    <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px"><strong>Where to look now:</strong> the
      <b>SANDF Documentation Centre, Pretoria</b> (Personnel Archives and Reserves, 20 Visagie Street),
      which holds Union Defence Force <b>attestation papers, personnel files and pension files</b> from
      1912. <b>None of it is online</b>, and it needs a written request. Note also that the CWGC site is
      behind a bot check and was not searched, and that FamilySearch's South African Defence collection is
      browse-by-image and not name-indexed.</p>
    <p style="font-size:16px;margin-top:12px"><span class="chip doc">Request sent, 9 Sept 2026</span>
      A letter has gone to the Documentation Centre at both published addresses, asking three things:
      whether a <b>pension file exists in her name</b>; <b>on whose service</b> it rested; and whether any
      of her eight named sons appears in <b>UDF attestation or personnel records</b> for 1914–18 or
      1939–45, and whether an attestation <b>names his mother as next of kin</b>.
      See <span class="m">requests/sandf-documentation-centre-pension.md</span>.</p>
    <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">The strength of the request is that it runs the search
      <em>backwards</em>. We have no service number for any son — but we know there <b>was</b> a pension,
      and we know the pensioner's name, her exact address and her date of death. The pension registers can
      be searched on those.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="lab"><span class="m">§ 14</span> · High priority</div>
    <h3 style="margin:8px 0 10px">Did any of the Bond Street money reach the Cape?</h3>
    <p style="font-size:16px">Johan Gottlieb Kolbe's 1842 will divides his residue among his children,
      with <b>the issue of any dead child taking that child's share</b>, and a later clause names
      <b>"my said children or grandchildren"</b> outright. <b>George Augustus Kolbe died eleven months
      before his father.</b> On paper his share therefore fell to his children at Burgersdorp — including
      Margaretha Kolbe, who married John Augustus Barry.</p>
    <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px"><strong>Where to look:</strong> the PCC estate and accounting
      papers behind the 1846 probate (TNA, PROB series); the Cape estate files for Margaret Downing
      (estate 4094, 1868) and for the Barry marriage settlement; and any correspondence between
      <b>Timothy Duke of Lombard Street</b> and the Cape.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="lab"><span class="m">§ 13</span> · Medium priority</div>
    <h3 style="margin:8px 0 10px">Test the deeper Kolbe and Downing pedigrees</h3>
    <p style="font-size:16px">The tree carries Kolbe back to <b>1699</b> in Württemberg and Downing back to
      <b>1683</b>, through the surnames <b>Jung</b>, <b>Larman</b>, <b>Trick</b>, <b>Heard</b>,
      <b>Hooper</b> and <b>King</b>. None of it has been tested, and Margaret Downing's own death notice
      names <em>neither</em> of her parents.</p>
    <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px"><strong>Where to look:</strong> London parish registers for
      St Anne's Soho and St Leonard's Shoreditch; Devon parish registers for Trick, Heard and Hooper;
      Württemberg Lutheran church books for Kolbe and Jung.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>The last version of this page said one document would change more than any amount of further
    reasoning — the death record of Willem Hermanus Booyzen. It was found, and it did.</b> It gave a date
    (13 September 1905), a place (Doorn Street, Indwe), a cause (ulcer of the stomach, fourteen months), an
    occupation (mason), an informant (his eldest son), and — four lines apart on one page — <b>both
    spellings of the surname</b>. <a href={u("/documents/")}>The documents &rarr;</a><br /><br />
    It had been sitting in the family tree's own photo store, unread, the whole time. That is the lesson
    worth keeping: <b>look at what you already have before you go looking for more.</b>
  </div>
</section>
